site/demos -- demos as code
===========================

A demo is ONE Python file that is both a gate and a screencast.

    make check-demo-fedgrep    run it as assertions (the sweep does this)
    make check-demos           every demo in this directory
    make demos                 record every demo to build/demos/ (Mac,
                               release time; needs ffmpeg; not in any sweep)

Because the film is made by the file the sweep runs, a demo cannot rot
unnoticed: it goes red in the sweep before anyone films it broken
(docs/SITE.md sec 3, docs/RECORDER.md).

The two modes
-------------

GATE MODE (scripts/demo_record.py DEMO.py). Runs on the person-like
driver scripts/e2e_term.py (class Term) over verilogapple's MCP, on a
copy-on-write copy of build/play.po. say(), hold() and fast() do
nothing; the expect_* calls are the assertions. No screen_image, no
ffmpeg, so it runs on the sweep box.

RECORD MODE (--record OUTDIR). The same file, on a Term whose every step
is a whole video frame (34,016 cycles = 1/30 s of emulated time) with one
screen_image after it. Key gaps are a seeded 3-5 frames (100-167 ms), so
a recording is a deterministic function of (demo, seed, image, floppy,
verilogapple build). A failed expect_* aborts the recording and leaves
nothing in OUTDIR. Output, under OUTDIR/NAME/:

    NAME.mp4  NAME.webm   2x upscale of a 720x468 frame: a 720x432
                          picture area (the //e's 560x384 centred in it,
                          the card's picture filling it) with a 36-row
                          key strip BELOW it
    poster.png            the screen at t.poster() (default: the first
                          say, else the last frame)
    NAME.vtt              the say() captions, timed in emulated seconds
    transcript.txt        the settled screen at every expect_*
    frames.sha256         one hash per output frame (the determinism
                          oracle; check-recorder compares these)

The machine is the same in both modes and on every run: the Timemaster
clock is pinned OFF (reset clock:false), because verilogapple seeds it from
the host's wall clock at every cold reset and a recording made a minute
later would otherwise start from a different time. So `date` says "no valid
clock reading" and `ls -l` shows no mtime; do not film either.
`demo_record.py --host-clock` turns the card back on, for measurements.

The strip shows every key sent for about a second: printable keys grouped
into words, control keys named (RET, ESC, TAB, DEL, ^C, ^Z ...) in amber.
Inside fast(n) it also shows an xN badge and the emulated cycle count.
Crisp pixels only: screen_image has no CRT mode. The //e's 560x384
picture sits between 80-pixel black pillars of a 720-wide frame; the
UltraTerm's picture (backend 'ut', below) fills it.

A demo file
-----------

    DEMO = dict(
        name='fedgrep',              # must equal the file name
        title='grep, written in vi',
        backends=('text80', 'ut'),   # the displays it is filmed and gated
                                     # on: text40, text80, ut (the Videx
                                     # UltraTerm); backend='text80' is the
                                     # one-element form
        image='fed',                 # play, full (the release disk: nas,
                                     # ld, make, liba; hello.py), or a
                                     # build_demo_images.py disk
        floppy='ddshrink',           # OPTIONAL: a 5.25" floppy in slot 6
                                     # drive 1 (recorder FLOPPIES table)
        gate='check-demo-fedgrep',   # must be check-demo-<name>
    )

    def run(t):
        ...

The Makefile builds check-demo-<name> from the files in this directory;
adding site/demos/foo.py adds check-demo-foo and puts it in check-demos
with no Makefile edit. The first thing run(t) sees is a settled shell
prompt. Do not call boot(): the harness did, and `boot(c, 'text80')`
ends on a check that matches the echoed `$ vidmode 80` line
(learning:01M3KY2AS0XJ22DY89S7R728GC), which the harness follows with
t.wait_prompt().

The demo API, all methods of `t`
--------------------------------

From Term (scripts/e2e_term.py; read docs/E2E-TERM.md first):
    press(key)  type_human(s)  enter()  wait(s, label)  wait_prompt(label)
    run_cmd_echoed(cmd, label)    wait for the prompt, type cmd (it must
                                  echo), RET. The expect_* below read
                                  the output of THIS command.
    CTRL_C CTRL_D CTRL_Z ESC      key constants ('\x0c' is ^L, a
                                  clean screen: there is no clear(1))

Added by the recorder:
    type_line(text, label)        type text a key at a time, require the
                                  echo, press RET (for a here-doc body)
    say(text, over=False)         a caption BEAT: the cue opens, the screen
                                  holds with no key for the reading time
                                  (1.2 s + 55 ms a char, 2.4-6.5 s), the
                                  cue closes, 0.45 s of quiet, then your
                                  next line runs. Never type under a
                                  caption: a key sent while a cue is open
                                  raises RecorderError (gate and record).
                                  over=True narrates a wait with no typing
                                  (a compile): it returns at once, may
                                  overlap output, and endcue() waits it
                                  out before the next key. Not inside fast()
    hold(seconds)                 dwell: record mode steps the machine,
                                  so the cursor and the clock stay honest
                                  (hold(0) steps nothing)
    fast(n)                       context manager: keep one frame in n,
                                  badge and cycle counter on each kept
    poster()                      the next frame is the poster
    popover(text, seconds)        THE ONE OVERLAY ON THE PICTURE, by the
                                  operator's ruling (2026-10-20): hold
                                  `seconds`, with a small bordered box
                                  of `text` over the lower right of the
                                  Apple screen. Call it last. Never in
                                  the poster or the captions. Gate mode:
                                  hold(), after the same refusals as
                                  record mode: a character the 5x7 font
                                  lacks raises, and so does a popover in
                                  fast(). fedgrep.py ends with one.
    output(label)                 the settled output rows of the last
                                  run_cmd_echoed command, as a list
    expect_lines(spec, label)     spec is a list of rows (content AND
                                  order) or an int (a row count)
    expect_text(text, label)      the same, rows given as one string
    expect_cmp_silent(label)      the command printed nothing
    expect(cond, label)           a plain assertion that names itself

"Settled" means the prompt is back and two polls in a row are identical
(a paused-CPU snapshot can catch a scroll half done,
learning:01M1AH9XWGC8ZS7HNBJVJ2JHXW). The assertions never depend on
slice size, which is why both modes are gated
(learning:01M1QY8TDRAX1N92CZQ143Q614). They check content and order, not
row membership: a tool whose failure is "print its input unchanged" passes
a membership test (learning:01M317VKBTQPCDM2DNMD36N1QV), so assert the
rows, or the count, or the silence.

Worked example: fedgrep.py (W3, docs/DEMO-FEDGREP.md)
----------------------------------------------------

grep is named for the ed command g/re/p. The demo types a six-line shell
script (a here-doc handed to ed) into vi, `cat`s it back and requires the
six lines it typed, in order; shows the Federalist Papers on the image
(`ls | wc -l` is 85, `head` of No. 10 says MADISON); runs the script on
No. 10 and requires exactly the lines the HOST finds holding the word in
the same bytes (scripts/demo_host.py reads the image the guest booted a
copy of); runs the real grep and requires the SAME rows; captures both
to files and requires `cmp` to print nothing; then does the same over
the papers headed MADISON with xargs, under fast(), and ends with the
operator's popover. The script is plain text, no macros.

Its teeth, seen red: /bin/ed, /bin/grep or /bin/cmp replaced by a copy
of /bin/cat each turn the gate red at the row that uses it. To try one
by hand:

    python3 scripts/demo_record.py site/demos/fedgrep.py \
        --setup 'cp /bin/cat /bin/grep'

--setup types its command at the shell before the demo starts, unfilmed;
the mount is copy-on-write, so the image is untouched.

`--floppy PATH` puts that file in slot 6 drive 1 instead of the demo's
own DEMO['floppy'] (a flipped byte, a truncated disk). The recorder
mounts a floppy BEFORE boot() (boot's reset keeps it; mounting after a
text40 boot's pokes would cold-reset them and boot native 80), checks the
mount reply, and hands the demo the path it mounted as `t.floppy`, so a
demo can compare the file actually in the drive with the host's oracle.
The mount is copy-on-write: the file is never written.

The recorder's own tests use fixtures/demos/mygrep.py, the earlier film
of the same joke (a five-line script typed with `cat >`, run on grep's
manual page). It lives there, not here, so the site shows one film of
the joke; check-recorder's rows and teeth on it are unchanged.

Adding a demo
-------------

1. Write site/demos/NAME.py (above). Pick the backend on purpose: at true
   speed a scroll is 15k cycles a line on text40 and 40k on native 80
   (learning:01M1JNVYS4E6K0DX5CCSJYD5GE), and that is what a viewer sees.
2. `make check-demo-NAME` until green; then break the subject once and
   watch it go red (rename a command on the demo's image with --setup).
3. `python3 scripts/demo_record.py site/demos/NAME.py --record /tmp/x
   --no-encode` is the fast way to look at the transcript and captions;
   `make demos` encodes.
4. Keep it ASCII (check-ascii covers this directory).
5. Everything under build/demos/ is generated and is never committed.

Freshness stamps and film sizes (docs/RECORDER.md "Freshness stamps")
--------------------------------------------------------------------

`make demos` also writes NAME.stamp.json (NAME-ut.stamp.json for the
card) beside each film: sha256 of this script, of the disk image it
booted, of every recorder module it loaded (demo_host.py and the rest),
of the verilogapple binaries and ROMs, the ffmpeg version, and of the
film's own files. `make check-site` and `make site` (whenever build/demos
holds films) recompute them and fail a film whose script, image or any of
those changed since it was recorded, a film with no stamp, a stamp that
belongs to another film, a demo without its films once any exist, and any
mp4, webm or poster whose size is not the one size the recorder declares
(scripts/demo_stamp.py). So editing a demo script, or demo_host.py, means
re-recording: `make demos`. The stamps are generated files under
build/demos/ and are never committed.

Two displays (docs/DEMO-UT.md, docs/DEMO-UT-NOTES.md)
-----------------------------------------------------

Every demo here declares backends=('text80', 'ut'): the //e's native 80
columns and the Videx UltraTerm. ONE does not, and it is the only kind
of exception there is: lssys.py crosses a `bye` window, and during one
the card is not the selected display at all (byelaunch.s hands the
glass back to the motherboard; measured in docs/DEMO-W17-NOTES.md), so
a card film would freeze on the farewell for the whole tenant run and
an expect_* taken there would read leftover maux text. A demo that
declares one display says why, in its own docstring. The card has its own video, which
screen_image does not draw, so the recorder draws it on the host from
ultraterm_state (CRTC registers, the $C0B3 attribute latch, 4 KB of VRAM)
through the card's own character ROM: scripts/demo_ut.py, proved against
verilogapple's renderer by scripts/check_ut_render.py. The phosphor is
green (0x40FF30, fast_sdl's colour for the card); the //e is white.

    make check-demo-NAME     gates NAME on EVERY display it declares, then
                             requires the same keys and the same text
    make demos               records every display: NAME.mp4 NAME.webm
                             poster.png NAME.vtt transcript.txt for the
                             //e, NAME-ut.mp4 ... poster-ut.png ... for
                             the card, side by side in build/demos/NAME/
    demo_record.py --backend ut DEMO.py     one display only

SAME KEYS, SAME TEXT is a rule, not a convention: the keys sent and the
settled screen at every expect_* must be identical on both displays, or
the gate is red and names the first difference. So run(t) must not know
the display. Two things differ by nature and are handled by the harness
and not by forking a demo: the //e boot types `vidmode 80` and the card's
does not (so the card's boot runs `true`, to spend the same PID, and both
clear the screen, unfilmed), and the MCP's screen_text decodes an inverse
cell on the //e's 80 columns wrongly (inverse `sh` reads as `3(`), which
the recorder's own screen() corrects from the text pages. Every checkpoint
is the raw machine screen: nothing is ever substituted for what was
scraped, and there is no way to pass the rows a checkpoint should hold.

Not here: the asciinema cast and the site generator.

Font licence: scripts/demo_font.py is an original 5x7 font drawn for this
repository (MIT), traced from nothing and containing no Apple ROM data.
